<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
        <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
        <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
    </div>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Discovering Our Classroom</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What's in Your Bag?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow oral directions for simple games. Interactive: Present responses based on listened questions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow game directions to place or find objects accurately (e.g., "Put the pencil in the bag."). Can create and share responses about object locations (e.g., "What's on your desk?" "A notebook."). Can listen and respond to questions about object locations (e.g., "Where is the book?" "On the table.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Simon Says" game using common classroom objects. The teacher holds up realia (pencil, book, ruler, eraser) and gives commands. For example: "Simon Says, point to the pencil!" "Simon Says, hold your book!" "Simon Says, put your ruler on your head!" This activates listening and introduces core vocabulary.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of 8-10 core classroom objects (pencil, book, notebook, ruler, eraser, crayon, pen, pencil case) and prepositions of place (in, on, next to) using realia and large flashcards. For example, the teacher places a pencil *in* a pencil case, *on* a book, and *next to* a notebook, clearly stating the action and object. Special attention is given to the pronunciation of /b/ in "book" and the blending of /ɔ/ /-n/ for "on".</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using visuals and gestures.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher holds up a pencil) "Is this a ruler or a pencil?" (Expected student response: "A pencil!")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher puts a book *under* the table) "Is the book *on* the table?" (Expected student response: "No!") Then, (Teacher puts the book *on* the table) "Is the book *on* the table?" (Expected student response: "Yes!")</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario:</b> "Discovering Our Classroom".</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, simple audio dialogue or a teacher-read dialogue between two students about classroom objects and their location. Flashcards showing a bag, desk, and various classroom items (pencil, book, notebook, ruler, pencil case, crayon). A large picture or drawing of a classroom.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Dialogue:</b>
                        <br>Teacher: "Listen to the dialogue. What do Ana and Tom talk about?"
                        <br>Ana: "Hi Tom! What's in your bag?"
                        <br>Tom: "Hi Ana! I have a book, a pencil, and an eraser in my bag."
                        <br>Ana: "Oh, where is your pencil case?"
                        <br>Tom: "My pencil case is on my desk."
                        <br>Ana: "And where is your notebook?"
                        <br>Tom: "My notebook is next to my book, in my bag."
                    </p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Locating objects and places, confirming object locations, and using appropriate prepositions to describe familiar object places (e.g., "The book is on the table.").</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "Who talks about things in their bag?" (Ana and Tom)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Point:</b> The teacher calls out objects/locations from the dialogue, and students point to the corresponding flashcard or part of the classroom picture. E.g., "Point to the pencil!" "Point to the desk!"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False statements:</b> The teacher makes statements based on the dialogue, and students respond "True" or "False" by showing a thumbs up/down. E.g., "Tom has a pen in his bag. True or False?" (False) "Tom's pencil case is on his desk. True or False?" (True)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Accuracy focus on Listening.</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for WH-questions ("What's in your bag?", "Where is the...?"), Prepositions of place (in, on, next to), Present simple ("The book is..."), and core vocabulary (pencil, book, notebook, ruler, eraser, crayon, pen, pencil case). Pronunciation focus on /b/ in "book" and /t/ in "notebook", and blending for "on".</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice: "Listen and Number"</b>
                                <br>Students receive a worksheet with pictures of various classroom objects and places (desk, bag, shelf) with blank circles. The teacher reads out descriptions, and students listen and number the corresponding picture.
                                <br>Example: "Number 1: a book on the desk." "Number 2: a pencil in the pencil case." "Number 3: an eraser next to the ruler."
                            </li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled: "Listen and Draw/Color"</b>
                                <br>Students have a worksheet with an empty desk and bag outline. The teacher gives oral instructions using target vocabulary and prepositions. Students listen and draw/color the objects in the specified locations.
                                <br>Example: "Draw a red book *on* the desk." "Draw a green pencil *in* the pencil case." "Draw an eraser *next to* the ruler *on* the desk." This incorporates adjectives and verbs (color, draw).
                            </li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction: "Listen and Place"</b>
                                <br>Students work in pairs with a small collection of realia (their own pencil, book, eraser, ruler) or object flashcards. One student (or the teacher) gives simple commands, and the other student listens and places the objects accordingly.
                                <br>Example: "Put the pencil *in* your pencil case." "Place the eraser *on* your book." "Put your ruler *next to* your notebook." The focus is on the listener following the directions accurately.
                            </li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario:</b> "Discovering Our Classroom".</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> "Classroom Scavenger Hunt - Listening Edition". Students work in small groups (2-3 students). The teacher hides several classroom objects (e.g., a *red crayon*, a *small eraser*, a *blue pen*, a *large book*) around the classroom.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme:</b> "What's in Your Bag?" and Scenario: "Discovering Our Classroom". The task involves identifying and locating classroom items using learned vocabulary and prepositions.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: disc;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives the first oral clue to all groups. E.g., "Listen carefully! Find the *red crayon*. It is *on* the *shelf*, *next to* the *board*." (Teacher pronounces /b/ in 'board' clearly).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The groups listen and work together to find the object based on the oral directions.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The first group to correctly find the object brings it to the teacher. They then receive the next oral clue for another hidden object.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The game continues until all objects are found. The focus is purely on listening comprehension and following multi-step directions accurately.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation during the "Listen and Organize Your Desk" activity, using an informal checklist for accuracy in following directions and identifying objects.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> "Listen and Organize Your Desk". Each student has their school supplies on their desk (book, pencil, notebook, eraser, pencil case, ruler). The teacher gives a series of oral commands for students to arrange their items.
                        <br>Example: "Put your *book* *on* your desk." "Place your *pencil* *in* your *pencil case*." "Put your *eraser* *next to* your *ruler*." "Where is your *notebook*? Put it *in* your *bag*."
                        <br>The teacher walks around to observe students' responses.
                    </p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: disc;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in following oral directions (e.g., placing the correct object in the correct location).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identification of target vocabulary objects (pencil, book, notebook, ruler, eraser, pencil case).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attentive listening during the activity.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: disc;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can follow directions like 'Put the book *on* the desk.' Yes / No"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I learned new words today! I can say: ___________ (students write 1-2 words they learned, e.g., 'pencil', 'on')"</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt (2 guiding questions for learners):</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: disc;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to listen to today in our class?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was difficult for me to understand when listening?"</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: disc;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this listening lesson? (e.g., TPR activities, clarity of pronunciation, engagement with realia).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with following directions or identifying specific vocabulary? (e.g., more visual aids, repetition, slower pace, simpler commands).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the phonemic awareness activities (e.g., /b/ in "book", blending "on") effective?</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b>
                        <br>"Great job listening and finding our classroom objects today! Next lesson, we will *read* about what's in a school bag and practice understanding sentences with our new words."
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>